WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:05.200
With major blockbusters and huge franchises
taking up most of our attention these days,

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:10.099
it can be easy to lose track of all the great
releases sneaking by under the radar but these

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:12.999
2018 fantasy movies are well worth seeking
out.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.910
For his feature directorial debut, Anders
Walter has successfully brought Joe Kelly's

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:23.080
graphic novel I Kill Giants to life, resulting
in a mesmerizing tale of heartache and heroism.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.560
The plot follows Barbara Thorson, a restless
12-year-old girl living on Long Island who,

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.960
in an effort to escape from her turbulent
home life, takes on a unique hobby: slaying

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:32.460
giants.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.340
“Giants aren’t real.”

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.120
“Then why are you sweating?”

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:41.020
As the film follows Barbara through her adventures,
it beautifully weaves together childhood heartache

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:44.920
and thrilling escapism along with an epic
dose of fantasy action.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.640
With solid directing, acting and CGI, this
all-around high-quality feature has a kind

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:54.000
of Pan's Labyrinth feel to it, and is bound
to appeal to your inner giant slayer.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.700
Although most blockbusters are plenty of fun
and full of eye candy, there's still something

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:02.179
special about a well-made movie that didn't
have access to mountains of cash.

00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:06.890
Case in point: Justin Benson and Aaron Moorhead's
The Endless, a not-quite-sequel to their first

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:07.760
movie, Resolution.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:11.940
The duo directed, shot and starred in the
film, delivering a shocking fantasy-tinged

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:16.080
story of uneasy fear which will discomfort
even the most stalwart horror fan.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.080
The film's storyline follows Justin and Aaron
Smith as they return to the mysterious cult

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:21.000
they once escaped.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.520
Once there, the unnatural peace is slowly
and masterfully broken down in a series of

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:28.820
revelations that expose a horrific, Lovecraftian
underbelly.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.840
“Hey I drew you a going away gift.

00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:32.960
Aww… wow.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:33.840
Thank you.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:38.200
it’s honest.”

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:42.800
Tastefully terrorizing, The Endless blends
elements of fantasy, sci-fi and horror, all

00:01:42.801 --> 00:01:47.200
without the advantages of a huge budget or
A-list actors not that this stopped the film

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.660
from attracting near-universal critical acclaim.

00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.500
How do you think you gain telekinetic powers?

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:56.040
Obviously, by drinking water from a mountain
spring hit by a meteor.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.950
At least that's what Train to Busan’s director
Sang-ho Yeon had in mind when he made one

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:03.020
of the most refreshingly unusual superhero
movies of the year.

00:02:03.020 --> 00:02:07.980
Written and directed by Yeon, Psychokinesis
is a good reminder that superhero movies "don’t

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:09.300
have to be about saving the world."

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:14.740
The story is inwardly focused, following the
origin of the unlikely hero Seok-heon, an

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:17.420
ordinary man who accidentally gains telekinetic
powers.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.200
This all happens just in time to receive a
distress call from his estranged daughter

00:02:21.209 --> 00:02:25.909
Roo-mi, informing him that a mob-run construction
company is threatening her trendy fried chicken

00:02:25.909 --> 00:02:26.820
restaurant in Seoul.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:31.260
Launched into the action as he's still just
getting to grips with his own abilities, Seok-heon

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:35.939
comes across as a perfectly illegitimate superhero,
frustrated by his own powers and often unable

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:36.920
to think on his feet.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:41.760
Simple and refreshingly light, it's a superhero
movie of down-to-earth proportions and a welcome

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.400
change of pace in a Marvel and DC-dominated
world.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:49.360
Before the turn of the millennium, director
Terry Gilliam set out to film an adaptation

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.900
of Miguel de Cervantes' classic 1615 novel
Don Quixote.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:56.900
His attempts to do so became the stuff of
Hollywood legend, as Gilliam battled through

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:02.220
a seemingly endless series of production delays,
actor turnovers and legal and financial fiascos.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:06.959
His journey even inspired its own documentary,
2002's Lost In La Mancha, which followed the

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.340
futility of an early go-around on the project
that featured Johnny Depp as Sancho Panza.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:15.740
But Gilliam's vision for his comically romanticized
film was set with gritty determination, and

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:20.300
decades later the movie has finally arrived
in the form of The Man Who Killed Don Quixote.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:24.659
The film’s plot progresses through an absurd
and fantastic series of events, with Adam

00:03:24.659 --> 00:03:29.019
Driver playing a simple yet perfect foil to
Jonathan Pryce's wide-eyed, ludicrously charming

00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.860
shoemaker who believes he is the real Don
Quixote.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:35.760
It's a fun movie with a great story behind
it, which only serves to make it that much

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.900
more interesting to watch.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:42.540
If you're just as much a fan of romantic comedies
as you are of a good, shocking twist or two,

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:44.520
you're going to want to check out When We
First Met.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:48.560
The story follows Noah, a man who who falls
in love with a woman named Avery after the

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:53.069
two spend an evening romping around in a near-perfect
"date-like" series of fun adventures which

00:03:53.069 --> 00:03:55.060
ends with a soul-crushingly friendly hug.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:59.980
As time goes on, Noah pines for his lost love
until he stumbles into a fantastical photo

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.440
booth that sends him back in time to the day
they met.

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:07.000
This sets off a hilarious series of "try and
try again" scenarios in which Noah attempts

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:11.140
to change his personality in order to create
the perfect first date, and win Avery over

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:13.290
before she can meet her future fiancé Ethan.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:14.660
“They’re like love magnets.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.780
And no matter what happens they’re going
to smash into each other… over and over

00:04:18.780 --> 00:04:20.120
and over and over and over.”

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:20.620
“Okay.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:21.140
I get it.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:22.320
You’re extremely threatened by Ethan.”

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:26.960
When We First Met has a clear-cut Groundhog
Day feel to it, but Adam Devine's ingratiating

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:30.480
energy saves the movie, keeping the story
light and the audience laughing.

